    <section id="about-me" class="cols-md">
      <h2>About Me</h2>
      <section>
        <h3>My ShenDao Path</h3>
        <p>
          In Chinese medicine, Shen Dao refers to the "Way of the Spirit" or "Path of the Spirit." This term consists of two characters: Shen, meaning spirit, consciousness, or the psychological aspects of a person, and Dao, which translates to way or path.
        </p>
        <p>
          Chinese medicine emphasizes a holistic approach to health, focusing on the balance and harmony between body, mind, and spirit. Shen in this context refers not only to mental and emotional health but is also considered a key element influencing overall health and well-being. Dao is a moral pattern, a path that Heaven has imprinted in the human heart. Dao represents the right way of life, enabling us to realize our potential.
        </p>
        <p>
          My current work stems from my personal Dao path, filled with many bumps and challenges for which I am grateful, as they led me to Chinese Medicine, which has been with me for many years. Initially, I experienced it as a patient and since 2017 as a student. With joy, I can now support you on your journey towards better health, accompanying you with mindfulness and compassion for the difficulties you face, observing and listening.
        </p>
        <p>
          My goal is to provide prevention and support you in returning to health by activating the body's natural forces, allowing you to live life with lightness in heart, body, and mind. In my practice, I use acupuncture, fire cupping, gua sha, moxibustion, and TDP lamps. You can also expect dietary and herbal recommendations.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h3>My Knowledge</h3>
        <p>
          A therapeutic relationship full of trust, safety, and care is important to me.
        </p>
        <p>
          I am a member of the Polish Society of Professional Acupuncturists. I have completed the following training in acupuncture and natural therapies:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>The Meaning of Dreams in Chinese Medicine – dr Hamid Montakab, Institute of Chinese Medicine and Health Prevention, Kraków</li>
          <li>Managing Ancestral Trauma with Acupuncture – dr Hamid Montakab, Institute of Chinese Medicine and Health Prevention, Kraków</li>
          <li>Applied I Ching Acupuncture Level 2 – Jonathan Shubs, Traditional Acupuncture School, Bydgoszcz</li>
          <li>Gynecology According to Classical Chinese Medicine – Li Jie, Anna Czarzasta, Natalia Kołodziej-Wojciechowska, TOMO School, Gdańsk</li>
          <li>Unified Acupuncture Theory Applied I Ching Ba Gua Level 1 – Jonathan Shubs, Traditional Acupuncture School, Bydgoszcz</li>
          <li>Unified Acupuncture Theory Clinical Applications: Sa'am 4 Needles 5 Elements Using Abdominal Palpation – Jonathan Shubs, Healthy Seminars</li>
          <li>Treating Thoracic and Facial Problems with Entry and Exit Points – Jonathan Shubs, Healthy Seminars</li>
          <li>Pulse Diagnosis – Bożena Olszowska, ProHumane, Wrocław</li>
          <li>Pulse Diagnosis – Bartosz Chmielnicki, Orkiszowe Pola, Poznań</li>
          <li>Differential Diagnostics, Pulse, Tongue, and Face Diagnosis – Claude Diolosa, Avicenna Polska, Kraków/Assisi</li>
          <li>Psychological, Psychoemotional, and Spiritual Disorders According to TCM – Sohial Farzam, Traditional Acupuncture School, Bydgoszcz</li>
          <li>Unified Acupuncture Theory Foundations – Jonathan Shubs, Traditional Acupuncture School, Bydgoszcz</li>
          <li>3-year Michał Boym Traditional Acupuncture School, Bydgoszcz</li>
          <li>Integrating Western Herbal Medicine, Chinese Medicine, and Phytopharmacology – Jeremy Ross, TOMO, Gdańsk</li>
          <li>Professional Herbalist Course and Herbal-Medical Course in Herbalistics – Institute of Polish Herbalism and Natural Therapies, Warsaw</li>
          <li>Preventive and Therapeutic Dietetics Course According to Chinese Medicine – Agnieszka Krzemińska, Orkiszowe Pola, Poznań</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The most valuable lessons come from meeting another person.</p>
